    {
      "id": "hdmtjpg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Ocean Waves – Meaning and Characteristics"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Waves are ocean surface movements driven by wind energy where water particles move in circular paths while energy travels forward.",
        "Tides are the periodic rise and fall of sea level caused mainly by the gravitational pull of the Moon and Sun along with centrifugal force.",
        "Spring tides occur during full and new moons, while neap tides occur during the first and third quarter moon phases.",
        "Tsunamis are seismic sea waves caused by earthquakes, volcanic eruptions, or landslides, not by wind energy.",
        "Both tides and waves play crucial roles in navigation, port operations, fisheries, tidal energy generation, and coastal ecosystems."
      ],
      "id": "5kg4t3u"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Ocean waves are oscillatory movements of water particles caused mainly by wind energy transferred to the ocean surface. In waves, water particles move in circular motion but the water itself does not travel long distances.",
      "id": "60cexy0"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "• Generated mainly by wind speed, duration, and fetch (distance over which wind blows).",
      "id": "s1pbfgh"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "• Energy moves forward but water particles move in circular paths.",
      "id": "rv4zr0s"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "• Wave height increases during storms.",
      "id": "7glzvkx"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "• Tsunamis are seismic waves, not wind-generated waves.",
      "id": "woaynq9"
    },
    {
