    {
      "id": "ywh5snb",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Types of Waves"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "• Wind Waves – Generated by surface winds.",
      "id": "p49rh6t"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "• Storm Waves – Higher waves during cyclones.",
      "id": "zb7baj6"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "• Tsunami – Caused by earthquakes, volcanic eruptions, or landslides.",
      "id": "u7mmqpu"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "• Tidal Waves – Misleading term; actual tides are gravitational.",
      "id": "o7g0x19"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "The Moon exerts a stronger tidal force due to its proximity to Earth.",
      "id": "gr2xot5"
    },
    {
      "id": "r0m11gg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Tides – Meaning and Causes"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Tides are the periodic rise and fall of ocean water levels caused primarily by the gravitational pull of the Moon and secondarily by the Sun. The Moon exerts a stronger tidal force due to its proximity to Earth.",
      "id": "id4szfa"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "• High tide – When sea level rises.",
      "id": "42ezali"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "• Low tide – When sea level falls.",
      "id": "fq389al"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "• Occurs twice daily in most coastal areas.",
      "id": "5zi5ofw"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "• Centrifugal force due to Earth-Moon rotation also contributes.",
      "id": "nxt9bmy"
    },
    {
      "id": "4n9g3vc",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Mechanism of Tide Formation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "On the side of Earth facing the Moon, gravitational pull causes water to bulge outward (high tide). On the opposite side, centrifugal force creates another bulge. Areas between these bulges experience low tide.",
      "id": "5amadnq"
    },
